wer":"C (108)","reason":"By central symmetry, take centroid G=(1,1), with circumradius R. At each triangle vertex, xy=1 gives R²=(x+y)²-2(x+y). Thus the three sums x+y take two quadratic-root values s,t, with one occurring twice, since a fixed sum allows at most two points on xy=1. Hence 2s+t=6 and s+t=2, giving s=4 and R²=8. The side squared is 3R²=24, so the area squared is 3·24²/16=108.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_238"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"hyperbola-symmetry-and-centroid","label":"Use symmetry and the centroid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hyperbola-symmetry-and-centroid":["cnm_238","cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_133","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_238","cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_133","cnm_h173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:654628ff626d099a1fef7145"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: x>1/2 or x<-1/3","reason":"For x>0, 1/x<2 gives x>1/2 and the other inequality is automatic. For x<0, 1/x>-3 gives 1<-3x, hence x<-1/3, while 1/x<2 is automatic.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_238"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1968_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reciprocal-graph","label":"Read the reciprocal graph","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-graph":["cnm_238"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_238"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:654628ff626d099a1fef7145"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 5/2 ≤ r < 3","reason":"Let g(t)=t³-2t²-2t-2. Then g(5/2)=-31/8 and g(3)=1, so an allowed polynomial has a root between them. For t≥3, any positive root would require 1≤2/t+2/t²+2/t³≤26/27, which is impossible. Hence the largest feasible root lies in the stated interval.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeId